  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Jacob Lopez gave them one good start. Five runs earned through 5.1. But the bats left fourteen on the field.",
    "what_this_means": "Boston scored late when it mattered. Heim and Butler each went deep, each drove in a run, and it still wasn't close to enough. Fourteen strikeouts against eight hits tells you everything: the lineup went quiet when it needed volume. One run total. That was the ceiling tonight. Lopez held the door open. Nobody walked through it. Empty innings in the first, fifth, seventh, and ninth. That's not randomness, that's a pattern of being close to the moment but not quite reaching it. The bullpen stayed clean across 4.2 innings (Elvis Alvarado with two strikeouts, Brady Basso holding tight), but clean relief doesn't matter when your offense can't finish basic math. This is what 45-63 looks like: good enough in the parts, broken when it's assembled.",
    "what_to_watch": "Sonny Gray's 2.78 ERA arrives tonight at 6:40 PT. Boston's starter is dangerous when Oakland's bats are already cold.",
